<%# The row of chips changed. Only the row is re-rendered: the transcript above
    it and a half-typed question below it have nothing to do with this choice,
    and replacing the whole panel body would throw both away.

    The ROW and not a single chip, for two reasons that are really one. A write
    here can change both chips at once (the button in the ticket header sets the
    reference and the project together), and a chip that renders nothing leaves
    no element behind for the next replace to find. The row survives being
    empty, so the anchor holds. %>
<%# @reference_expanded: only the reference controller sets it, and only because
    it is the one caller that KNOWS this request was a reference write (§8.1).
    The scope chip renders through the same template and leaves it nil, so a
    change of "Sucht in" never unfolds a collection the user collapsed. %>
<% row = Neo::Chat::ScopeRowComponent.new(
     chat: @chat, current_user: current_user, current_organisation: @current_organisation,
     reference_expanded: @reference_expanded.present?
   ) %>
<%= turbo_stream.replace row.dom_id do %>
  <%= render row %>
<% end %>

<%# ONE template for both outcomes, because the row is re-rendered either way and
    a second template would be a second copy of that render. What separates them
    is the message: the two refusals set flash.now (AssistantChatsController#refuse_scope_change
    and AssistantChatReferencesController#refuse), the success paths do not.

    Without this a refusal was indistinguishable from a success on every Turbo
    path -- same stream, same snapped-back chips, no word anywhere. The offer of
    step 5b clicks straight into this. %>
<% if flash[:alert].present? %>
  <%= turbo_stream.update 'flash' do %>
    <%= render partial: 'layouts/flash' %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
